---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes.ts';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO_URL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="partners"
  title="Partners"
  description="The compact between twente.dev and the communities it lists, and how organisations support it: in writing, in public, in git."
>
  <p>
    Two kinds of organisation end up on this page: communities we list, and organisations that
    support an edition. Both deserve to know the terms before they agree to anything, so the terms
    are written down here rather than promised in an email.
  </p>

  <h2>twente.dev will not compete with what already exists</h2>
  <p>
    twente.dev exists to support and amplify the technology activity already happening in Twente. We
    will not duplicate or compete with communities or events that already exist. Concretely, and
    without exception:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>We will not run our own events on your night.</strong> The shared calendar exists partly
      so we can see your dates and avoid them.
    </li>
    <li><strong>We will not recruit your speakers</strong>, your organisers or your volunteers.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>We will not list you without asking</strong>, and we remove you the moment you ask:
      one message, no questions, no notice period.
    </li>
    <li><strong>We will not claim to represent you.</strong> twente.dev speaks for twente.dev.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>We will not approach your existing sponsors.</strong> If a company already supports your
      community, it is yours. We ask for in-kind help before cash, so we are not competing for the same
      small pool of regional budget.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We will not take your data.</strong> No member lists, no attendee lists, no scraping, no
      imports, in either direction.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your community stays yours</h2>
  <p>
    All planning and communication for your community continues to come from you. Being listed
    changes nothing about how you run your group: no exclusivity, no approval process, no house
    style, no obligation to promote anything of ours, and no expectation that you attend anything.
    Your event, listed by us, is credited to you and links to your source. Never rebranded.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A free listing.</strong> Free now, free later. We will never charge a community to be listed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A calendar feed you own</strong>, published as ICS on open formats rather than a
      platform's API. We cannot paywall it and we cannot take it away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Collision avoidance.</strong> In a region this size, the most useful thing a shared calendar
      does is stop two good evenings landing on the same Thursday.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Cross-promotion</strong>, when you want it and in the form you want it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What we ask in return costs nothing: permission to list you, a correction when we get a detail
    wrong, and ideally some way to know when your events are happening. No membership, no fee, no
    exclusivity.
  </p>

  <h2>Supporting an edition</h2>
  <p>
    Organisations support twente.dev with rooms, food, production and printing, in return for
    visible credit, never for attendee data, stage time or editorial influence. We ask for in-kind
    support before cash, and we would rather have one thing you can genuinely give than a number you
    have to justify. Amounts are agreed per edition; there is no rate card on this page because
    there should not be one until the model has survived a real conversation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sponsors do not get a talk slot. Speaking is decided by the open call, on the same terms for
    everyone, including people who work for organisations that support us.
  </p>

  <h2>Who pays for this</h2>
  <p>
    Ryan Grippeling funds twente.dev himself and runs it through his own one-person business
    (Webgrip). His employer, Code14, contributes time and a small marketing budget. There is no
    foundation behind it and none is planned for now: one person, in the open. None of them buys
    stage time, attendee data or editorial influence. The same rule applies to everyone, and most of
    all to the people closest to the project. After every edition we publish a report, including
    what did not work.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing this</h2>
  <p>
    This compact lives in <a href={REPO_URL}>the public repository</a> with its full history. Changes
    are proposed there and announced to every listed community before they take effect. If a change is
    one you cannot live with, the exit is the same one-click removal it always was.
  </p>
  <p>
    Want to be listed, or to support an edition? Email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. If you run a community, a listing is
    free and always will be. See <a href={routePath('communities', 'en')}>communities</a>.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
